{"meta":{"chapter_title":"Foundations of CBI and Design Tensions","title":"Portfolio Evidence Setup","description":"CAT 531 textbook section connecting portfolio evidence setup to Design Tension Studio map naming a classroom value conflict and negotiated decision, reader notes, support moments, and revision evidence.","course":"cat531-supplement","chapter":"01","section":"07","learning_objectives":["Analyze how the Design Tension Studio map naming a classroom value conflict and negotiated decision shows a defensible decision in CAT 531.","Distinguish artifact polish from evidence-based revision.","Document a bounded AI/software support move with accepted and rejected suggestions.","Use annotations, support requests, and revision notes to make a classroom technology decision more defensible."],"concept_ids":["cat531_supplement_foundations_of_cbi_and_design_tensions","cat531_supplement_portfolio_evidence_setup","evidence_based_revision","ai_supported_learning"],"prereq_section_ids":["cat531-supplement/01/06"],"practice_ids":["cat531_supplement_1_7_portfolio_evidence_setup_artifact_signal","cat531_supplement_1_7_portfolio_evidence_setup_annotation_signal","cat531_supplement_1_7_portfolio_evidence_setup_ai_boundary","cat531_supplement_1_7_portfolio_evidence_setup_calibration"],"estimated_time_minutes":14,"order":7,"top_tier_hardened":false},"content":"# Portfolio Evidence Setup\n\n![Portfolio Evidence Setup textbook visual](/course-art/deep-exemplars/cat531-supplement/01-07-portfolio-evidence-setup.png)\n\n## Why This Section Matters\n\nA portfolio is not a scrapbook of things you made; it is an argument that you can do something, addressed to a reader who was not there. The difference is whether each entry is verifiable. \"I learned a lot about equity\" is a feeling, and a reviewer cannot check a feeling. \"Here is the memo where I declined a tool after a six-essay equity check\" is evidence, and a reviewer can examine it, agree or disagree, and form a judgment. The whole value of a portfolio rises or falls on that distinction.\n\nThis section matters because the most common portfolio failure is sincere: the author genuinely grew, says so, and attaches nothing inspectable. Setting up portfolio evidence well means treating each competency claim as a hypothesis a skeptic could test, anchoring it to a specific artifact, and showing growth as a documented change between two artifacts rather than as a report of feeling more confident.\n\n## Learning Targets\n\n- Analyze how the Design Tension Studio map naming a classroom value conflict and negotiated decision shows a defensible decision in CAT 531.\n- Distinguish artifact polish from evidence-based revision.\n- Document a bounded AI/software support move with accepted and rejected suggestions.\n- Use annotations, support requests, and revision notes to make a classroom technology decision more defensible.\n\n## Opening Case\n\nA teacher sets up her module-1 portfolio entry with the line: \"I learned a lot about balancing efficiency and equity, and I feel more confident making tool decisions now.\" It is honest and it may even be true, but it attaches nothing a reviewer can verify. The claim of learning floats free of any artifact, so a skeptical reader has no way to test it. The entry documents a feeling, not a competency.\n\nWhat counts as evidence a reviewer can verify is the question this section answers. The fix is not more words about confidence; it is a named artifact the reviewer can open and judge.\n\n## Core Concept\n\nPortfolio evidence setup is the practice of making each competency claim verifiable by anchoring it to an inspectable artifact and demonstrating growth as a documented trajectory. It rests on three moves:\n\n- **Claim as a hypothesis.** State the competency as something a skeptic could test (\"I can balance efficiency against equity in a tool decision\"), not as a feeling (\"I feel ready\").\n- **Anchor to a specific artifact.** Point to the actual thing you produced (the adoption memo from 1.4), and label exactly which part of it demonstrates the competency. An anchor a reviewer cannot locate is not an anchor.\n- **Show growth as a delta between two artifacts.** Place an early artifact next to a later one and annotate the difference. Growth is the change a reader can see, not the change you assert. \"Week 1: justified a tool by 'students find it engaging.' Week 4: declined a tool after a quantified equity check.\" The contrast is the evidence.\n\nThe hard distinction here is between reflection and evidence. Reflection narrates an internal change; evidence exhibits an external trace that supports the narration. A strong entry uses reflection to point at evidence, never as a replacement for it. [TPACK](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Technological_pedagogical_content_knowledge) and critical edtech studies are useful as labels for what a chosen artifact demonstrates, but only if the artifact actually shows the reasoning, not if the anchor names a theory the artifact never used.\n\n<inline-check question=\"An entry reads: 'I feel more confident making tool decisions now.' Why can a reviewer not verify this?\" explanation=\"Confidence is an internal state no reader can audit. It is reflection, not evidence. To be verifiable, the claim must be stated as a testable competency and anchored to a specific, locatable artifact the reviewer can open and judge.\" options='[{\"text\":\"It describes an internal state with no inspectable artifact\",\"correct\":true},{\"text\":\"It uses the word confident, which is banned\",\"correct\":false},{\"text\":\"It is too short\",\"correct\":false},{\"text\":\"It names two artifacts instead of one\",\"correct\":false}]'></inline-check>\n\n## Worked Example\n\nScenario: the teacher from the opening case rebuilds her module-1 portfolio entry. The question is what counts as evidence a reviewer can verify. Walk it in four numbered moves.\n\n**Before (claim-only setup).** Her first entry reads:\n\n> Module 1 takeaway: I learned a lot about balancing efficiency and equity, and I feel more confident making tool decisions now.\n\n1. **Diagnose the gap.** This asserts learning and confidence but attaches nothing inspectable. A reviewer cannot verify the claim. It is reflection, not evidence.\n2. **Select an artifact to anchor it.** Choose the actual adoption memo she wrote in 1.4 where she declined an AI grader for multilingual students.\n3. **Tie the artifact to the competency.** Label it: \"Evidence of competency: balancing efficiency and equity. This memo shows me quantifying a 170-minute efficiency gain against a 6-essay equity check and choosing a bounded trade-off.\"\n4. **Show growth, not just a product.** Pair it with her week-1 memo that justified a tool by 'students find it engaging,' and annotate the shift: early reasoning rested on preference; later reasoning rested on quantified trade-offs.\n\n**After (evidence-anchored setup).** The revised entry reads:\n\n> Competency: balancing efficiency and equity. Early artifact (week 1): tool justified by 'students find it engaging.' Later artifact (1.4): tool declined after a 6-essay equity check showed it under-scored 4 of 6 multilingual writers, with the efficiency cost named. The change in my reasoning, from preference to quantified trade-off, is the evidence of growth a reviewer can inspect.\n\nThe setup is defensible because each claim is anchored to a named, inspectable artifact and growth is shown as a documented trajectory rather than a self-report of feeling more confident.\n\nMatch each portfolio move to the test it must pass before a skeptical reviewer would accept it as evidence.\n\n<sequence-builder mode=\"match\" items='[{\"term\":\"Claim as a hypothesis\", \"definition\":\"Stated so a skeptic could test it (I can balance efficiency against equity), not as a feeling (I feel ready)\"}, {\"term\":\"Anchor to a specific artifact\", \"definition\":\"Points to the exact file a reviewer can open and labels which part demonstrates the competency\"}, {\"term\":\"Show growth as a delta\", \"definition\":\"Pairs an earlier artifact with a later one and annotates the visible difference, not a single polished product\"}]'></sequence-builder>\n\n## Decision Rule\n\nWhen setting up an entry, lead with the competency claim, then the artifact that proves it, then the boundary or limit the artifact respected. A portfolio claim is justified only when it points to an inspectable artifact that improves a specific, namable competency, not when it reports a feeling.\n\nA defensible entry survives three quick tests:\n\n1. **Purpose check:** Is the claim stated as a testable competency rather than a feeling?\n2. **Evidence check:** Can a reviewer open the exact artifact you named and find the part you say demonstrates the competency?\n3. **Revision check:** Is growth shown as a labeled difference between an earlier and a later artifact, not as a confidence report?\n\nIf a reviewer would have to take your word for it, the entry is reflection, not evidence.\n\n<self-explain prompt=\"Explain why one polished, strong artifact can demonstrate a present capability but still cannot support a claim of growth.\" answer=\"A single artifact, however strong, is a snapshot: it shows what you can do now, but not that your reasoning changed. Growth is a trajectory, which requires at least two artifacts with the difference made explicit, for example a week-1 memo justifying a tool by 'students find it engaging' next to a 1.4 memo declining a tool after a quantified equity check. The reader can see the shift from preference to evidence-based trade-off. Without the earlier artifact, a reviewer has nothing to measure the later one against, so polish gets mistaken for growth when it only ever shows a present state.\"></self-explain>\n\n## Common Misreadings\n\nThe dominant error is confidence-as-evidence: \"I feel more prepared\" describes an internal state no reader can audit. A second is the unlocatable anchor, citing \"my work this module\" without pointing to a specific file the reviewer can open. A third is the polished single artifact mistaken for growth; one strong product shows a present capability but not a trajectory, and growth claims require two artifacts with the difference made explicit. Polish is not evidence; an inspectable delta is.\n\n## Textbook Walkthrough\n\nApply the setup to a different competency: \"I can match a tool to my classroom context rather than to a vendor's claim\" (the skill from 1.5). A teacher wants to evidence it.\n\nState the claim as a hypothesis a skeptic could test, not \"I think about context now.\" Then choose the anchor: the actual note where she compared a discussion-bot study's context (12 students, 1:1 devices, no multilingual learners) to her own (34 students, shared tablets, 9 multilingual learners) and decided to pilot locally.\n\nTie the artifact to the competency precisely: \"This note demonstrates context-matching because it lists the study's variables beside mine and suspends the borrowed result where they diverge.\" Then show growth by pairing it with an earlier note where she had written \"the study shows 30 percent, so I'll roll it out,\" annotating the shift from importing a number to interrogating its conditions. The walkthrough produces a verifiable entry for a different competency using the same three moves, demonstrating that the setup method, not the topic, is what transfers.\n\n## What Strong Reading Looks Like\n\nAs you read, keep a two-column note: each **competency claim** on the left, the **specific artifact a reviewer could open to verify it** on the right. If the right column says \"my growth\" or \"this module,\" the claim is unanchored. For this topic, the tell that you are reading well is that you can convert any feeling-statement into a testable claim plus a named artifact.\n\nA strong reader of portfolio setup can answer four questions:\n\n1. Is the claim a testable competency or a feeling?\n2. Which exact artifact, locatable by a reviewer, demonstrates it?\n3. Does the entry pair two artifacts to show growth as a visible delta?\n4. Would a skeptic be able to disagree with the evidence, which is the sign it is evidence at all?\n\n## Stronger Artifact Standard\n\nThe entry is strong when every competency claim names an inspectable artifact and growth appears as a labeled difference between an earlier and a later artifact. An entry that reports confidence or cites \"my work\" without a locatable file has supplied reflection in place of evidence; mark that gap and ask BigAL to compare a confidence-report entry against an artifact-anchored one rather than for a general explanation.\n\nA useful closing line sounds like: \"The change in my reasoning, from justifying a tool by 'students find it engaging' to declining one after a quantified equity check, is the evidence of growth a reviewer can inspect.\"\n\n## Transfer Case\n\nMove the portfolio setup into a high-stakes context: a National Board or licensure portfolio where an external assessor scores entries blind, with no access to you. The reliance on inspectable, locatable artifacts intensifies, because there is no conversation to fill the gaps your reflection leaves. If your setup method depends on being there to explain, it fails here; if it produces entries a stranger can verify cold, you understood evidence.\n\n## Artifact Studio\n\nOpen the studio packet: [download the Design Tension Studio map naming a classroom value conflict and negotiated decision packet](/downloads/summer2026/cat531-supplement/01-07-portfolio-evidence-setup-artifact.md).\n\n<artifact-studio artifact=\"Design Tension Studio map naming a classroom value conflict and negotiated decision\" course=\"CAT 531\" section=\"01.07\"></artifact-studio>\n\nUse the studio to create a before/after trace. The first version should show your starting interpretation. The revised version should show one meaningful change. The trace should explain why the change happened and why at least one possible suggestion was not accepted.\n\n## Adaptive Support\n\nALGET uses your annotations and artifact trace to choose support:\n\n- If your note is a **question**, ask for a concise explanation tied to **[TPACK](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Technological_pedagogical_content_knowledge)**.\n- If your note shows **confusion**, compare a weak and strong version of the Design Tension Studio map naming a classroom value conflict and negotiated decision.\n- If your note is an **insight**, transfer the same decision rule to a new audience or setting.\n- If your note makes a **connection**, show where the connection changes the artifact.\n\n<dynamic-scenario prompt=\"You are revising the Design Tension Studio map naming a classroom value conflict and negotiated decision for CAT 531 01.07. A peer comment flags that your growth claim rests on a single polished artifact with no earlier comparison. Choose the next revision move and the evidence that would show it helped.\"></dynamic-scenario>\n\n## Check Your Understanding\n\nBefore moving on, use the check to separate surface completion from a decision that another reader can inspect, question, and revise.\n\n<interactive-quiz question=\"Which trace would make the revision easiest to review?\" options='[\"A before-after artifact record linked to the decision you made and the evidence you used.\", \"The section gives a longer explanation and a more polished visual.\", \"The AI tool generates the artifact without requiring learner revision.\", \"The learner completes the page and receives a generic score.\"]' correct-index=\"0\" conceptid=\"cat531_supplement_portfolio_evidence_setup\"></interactive-quiz>\n\n## Final Check\n\nBefore you move on, check that the Design Tension Studio map naming a classroom value conflict and negotiated decision captures instructional purpose, equity or privacy boundary, support request, revision rationale, and confidence change.\n\n## Concept Map\n\nSee how verifiable, anchored claims connect to evidence-based revision and the course anchors.\n\n<concept-map concepts='[\"cat531_supplement_foundations_of_cbi_and_design_tensions\", \"cat531_supplement_portfolio_evidence_setup\", \"evidence_based_revision\", \"ai_supported_learning\"]'></concept-map>\n\n## References\n\n- [TPACK](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Technological_pedagogical_content_knowledge). https://doi.org/10.1111/j.1467-9620.2006.00684.x\n- critical edtech studies. https://doi.org/10.4324/9781315670160\n- [AI and education datafication](https://doi.org/10.1080/17439884.2020.1686017)\n- teacher professional judgment. https://doi.org/10.1177/002205741319300304\n","simulation":null,"illustration":null,"practice":{"problems":[{"id":"cat531_supplement_1_7_portfolio_evidence_setup_artifact_signal","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"cat531_supplement_portfolio_evidence_setup","stem":"A teacher's module-1 portfolio entry is a single line: 'I learned a lot about design tensions this week.' What makes this fail as portfolio EVIDENCE rather than reflection?","options":["It is too short to count.","It asserts learning but includes no artifact, decision, or trace a reviewer could inspect to verify the claim.","It mentions design tensions, which are off-topic.","It should have used the word 'evidence.'"],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Portfolio evidence must point to something inspectable: an artifact, a documented decision, a before/after trace. A bare claim of learning is reflection, not evidence.","misconception_id":"cat531_supplement_1_7_reflection_mistaken_for_evidence","difficulty":"easy","concept_ids":["cat531_supplement_foundations_of_cbi_and_design_tensions","cat531_supplement_portfolio_evidence_setup","evidence_based_revision","ai_supported_learning"]},{"id":"cat531_supplement_1_7_portfolio_evidence_setup_annotation_signal","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"cat531_supplement_portfolio_evidence_setup","stem":"A teacher uploads 14 lesson files to her portfolio with no labels or rationale, expecting volume to demonstrate competence. Why does this weaken the portfolio as evidence?","options":["Fourteen files is too many to store.","Unlabeled artifacts force the reviewer to guess what each one demonstrates; evidence requires each artifact to be tied to a specific claim or competency it supports.","Lesson files can never be portfolio evidence.","The files should have been combined into one document."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Quantity is not evidence. Each artifact must be linked to the claim it supports so a reviewer can see what competency it demonstrates, not infer it from a pile of files.","misconception_id":"cat531_supplement_1_7_volume_mistaken_for_evidence","difficulty":"medium","concept_ids":["cat531_supplement_portfolio_evidence_setup","evidence_based_revision"]},{"id":"cat531_supplement_1_7_portfolio_evidence_setup_ai_boundary","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"cat531_supplement_ai_supported_learning","stem":"A teacher asks an AI to 'write my portfolio reflection on this lesson.' The AI produces a fluent paragraph she submits unchanged. What is the integrity problem for a competency portfolio?","options":["AI text is always grammatically wrong.","The portfolio is meant to be evidence of HER reasoning and growth; submitting AI-authored reflection misrepresents whose judgment the evidence reflects.","She should have asked the AI for two paragraphs.","Portfolios may never mention AI at all."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"A portfolio certifies the teacher's own competency. AI may help organize or prompt thinking, but submitting AI-authored reflection as one's own evidence misrepresents the source of the judgment being assessed.","misconception_id":"cat531_supplement_1_7_ai_authored_evidence","difficulty":"medium","concept_ids":["ai_supported_learning","cat531_supplement_portfolio_evidence_setup"]},{"id":"cat531_supplement_1_7_portfolio_evidence_setup_calibration","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"cat531_supplement_portfolio_evidence_setup","stem":"A teacher wants her portfolio to show GROWTH in technology decision-making, not just a finished product. Which setup best supports that goal?","options":["Include only her best final artifact so the portfolio looks strong.","Pair an early decision artifact with a later revised one and annotate what changed in her reasoning and what evidence drove the change.","Include a long list of tools she has heard of.","Write one summary at the end describing how much she improved."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Growth is demonstrated by a documented trajectory: an early and a later artifact with an annotated account of the reasoning change. A single polished product or a self-report of improvement does not show the trajectory.","misconception_id":"cat531_supplement_1_7_best_work_mistaken_for_growth","difficulty":"hard","concept_ids":["cat531_supplement_portfolio_evidence_setup","evidence_based_revision"]}]},"content_version":{"content_version":"e1fca8b3e793d0c933a924fe71188462e0d218ee552f7f6f5fa3789506930f1d","algorithm":"sha256-mdx+meta+practice+misconceptions-v1","parts":["mdx","meta","practice","misconceptions"]}}